WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.235
-Hi. Oh, it's great to see --

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.670
-How are you?
-Hi, bud.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.071
-It's great to see you both.

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:08.074
You look fantastic.
Thank you for doing this.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.843
We have so much to talk about.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:12.745
I just watched the documentary
last night,

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:14.514
and I was blown away.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:19.518
"Siempre, Luis"
means "Always, Luis."

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:24.190
That is how you sign
all of your memos and letters?

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.393
-That's how I have signed
my entire life,

00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:29.829
and so I'm very honored

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:34.534
that then Lin-Manuel
took all the "Siempre, Luis."

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:36.769
So the "Siempre, Lin-Manuel."

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:40.105
So now it'll live forever.

00:00:40.105 --> 00:00:44.443
-It was -- The cameras
follow Luis around for a year.

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:45.678
And I was watching it.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.781
And I, you know --
I know you guys pretty well.

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:50.816
I thought Lin had
a lot of energy.

00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:54.186
Luis, you are nonstop.

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:57.857
-Well, that's what
some people say.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.492
I'm energetic

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:03.296
but by no means nonstop.

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:05.898
I'm working on being nonstop.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:09.536
-You're always doing something,
and it's -- I mean,

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.405
I know you have great energy
and just fun.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.406
But, wow, the work you put in.

00:01:14.406 --> 00:01:16.709
You came here from Puerto Rico,

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.645
and you came to New York City.

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:23.316
You worked on a number
of political campaigns.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:27.654
You've always worked
to help people in times of need.

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:29.088
You get that from watching this.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:33.426
And both of you are so active
in so many great causes.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:37.196
Lin, is that something
you learned from your dad?

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:38.598
-Yeah, I think so.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.301
It always just sort of
came with growing up.

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.835
Other kids had paper routes,

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:46.873
and I was registering voters
in buildings.

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.575
I was, like, the guy with the
green paper and the clipboard.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:52.846
That was my -- That was
my teenage chore of choice.

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:54.547
-Wow!
-So it was --

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.949
And then
I also remember, like,

00:01:56.949 --> 00:01:59.385
going to toy drives as a kid.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.888
We always did the Three Kings'
Day toy drive every year

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:07.926
where we would get toys for kids
for Three Kings' Day.

00:02:07.926 --> 00:02:11.230
So, yeah, there was always
sort of an activism

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.999
and sort of charity component
of growing up.

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:16.969
That just came with the deal
of living in our house.

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:20.907
-Luis, you were hoping
that Lin would be a lawyer.

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:22.809
Do you remember
the moment you knew

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.511
that he was going to
be a performer?

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:29.448
-Actually throughout my life,
I just pretended.

00:02:29.448 --> 00:02:32.017
-Look how he shakes his head.
Just so...

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:33.953
-I continued pushing.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:39.125
I figure I'll tire him out,
and he'll want to make me happy

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:43.129
and be like Rubén Blades
and also be a lawyer.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.566
But it was actually one day

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.668
watching him on TV

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:53.639
that he was with a chorus,
a school performance,

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:58.411
where it was so clear
to my wife, Luz, and me

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:02.114
that this kid
was not going to be a lawyer,

00:03:02.114 --> 00:03:06.919
as he was flapping his hands
and just being so animated.

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:08.687
-Yeah.
-I looked at Luz and said,

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:11.891
"He's never
going to be a lawyer."

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:14.793
Those skills of flapping hands
and stuff like that

00:03:14.793 --> 00:03:16.262
can be helpful for legal.

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:20.132
-Flapping hands? That's my --
That's what I do for a living.

00:03:20.132 --> 00:03:21.434
Flapping hands.
-"Your honor!

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:25.138
Your honor!
My client is innocent!"

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:26.973
-"I object!"

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.941
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:31.777
-I mean -- Ohh.
-It was clear.

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:34.413
It was not happening.
-Yeah.

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:36.082
Lin, you tell a story
in the documentary

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:38.117
about the time
you asked your dad for advice

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.118
about your career.

00:03:40.118 --> 00:03:41.787
What did you ask him?

00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:45.391
-Yeah. I was --
My first job out of college

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:48.126
was teaching 7th-grade English,

00:03:48.126 --> 00:03:51.030
and I found, to my surprise,
that I really liked it.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.065
I loved teaching.

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:57.903
It's an incredibly rewarding
profession.

00:03:57.903 --> 00:04:01.708
I think teachers should make,
like, all the money.

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:05.044
Like, I think we should just
reverse the way society works,

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:08.680
and teachers should get the
inverse of what they are making.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.183
And -- But I loved it.

00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:13.319
And I got offered
a full-time job

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.355
at the end of my first year,

00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:17.556
and I kind of saw
two paths ahead of me.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:20.626
I saw the
"Mr. Holland's Opus" path

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:23.462
where I live a happy life
teaching

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:27.032
but I never finish
that play I was working on.

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:29.935
And -- But I really --
I genuinely loved it.

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:32.871
And so I asked my dad,
"Should I take the full-time job

00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:36.876
or should I sort of say no
to that and not have a job

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:39.478
but, you know,
have more time to write?"

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:40.713
And he actually sort of

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:43.248
nudged me in the direction

00:04:43.248 --> 00:04:45.984
of pursuing my dream.

00:04:45.984 --> 00:04:48.087
And I'll always be grateful
to him for that,

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.455
even though he was,

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:52.124
you know, just on my shoulder
to be a lawyer

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:54.827
all through high school,
he kind of realized

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.864
you actually have to go
for the thing you want.

00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:00.533
-Luis, do you remember
writing that letter?

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:04.136
-I remember writing that letter.

00:05:04.136 --> 00:05:09.175
And the advice came easy.

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:12.444
I knew it was
the right thing to do,

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:15.848
even though
I was smacking myself

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:18.183
for giving that advice.

00:05:18.183 --> 00:05:20.686
-Flapping his arms!

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.888
-Parents should be saying,

00:05:22.888 --> 00:05:26.025
"Go to work, go to work,
take a job."

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:29.394
But I knew
that was not his path.

00:05:29.394 --> 00:05:31.997
He would've been
a great teacher.

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:34.200
He probably would have created

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.603
lots of Lin-Manuels
in that classroom,

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:42.808
but he would have not been
the Lin-Manuel we love.

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:48.047
-The one thing I took from it
is that the work is never over

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:51.584
and you just have to
just continue to wake up,

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.452
and you go, "Yeah,"
and you got to show up,

00:05:53.452 --> 00:05:54.987
and you got to show up
for people

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:57.523
and stand up for people.

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:00.592
It was inspiring to watch.

00:06:00.592 --> 00:06:02.961
Everyone should watch
"Siempre, Luis."

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:07.099
What do you want people
to take from it, Luis?

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:08.201
-Two things.

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:09.668
On the one hand,

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:12.905
that even though
the documentary is about me,

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:17.209
I'm really just a place holder
for migrants, immigrants

00:06:17.209 --> 00:06:18.644
who come into this country

00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:21.180
and have to work
the double shift

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.149
in order to move
their families forward.

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:27.720
And, two,
that my most important job,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.889
it's being a dad.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:34.093
That's how we live forever,
by being a good dad

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:36.528
and creating
the next generation,

00:06:36.528 --> 00:06:38.830
that it's better than you.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.667
-I want to show a clip.
Here's a look at "Siempre, Luis"

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:44.903
out on HBO and HBO Max
right now.

00:06:44.903 --> 00:06:46.271
Check this out.

00:06:46.271 --> 00:06:50.042
-If anything has come out
of Hurricane Maria

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:52.911
is that it has
brought together...

00:06:52.911 --> 00:06:57.115
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:06:57.115 --> 00:07:01.954
...our allies in the
United States with Puerto Rico.

00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:03.389
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:06.491
And now, together,

00:07:06.491 --> 00:07:09.328
we cannot be stopped!

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:10.496
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.631
-They're living in a hotel.

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:15.000
They're looking for a place.
They're looking for jobs.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.836
They're looking for schools
for their kids.

00:07:17.836 --> 00:07:20.139
And on top of that,
we're telling them,

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:21.440
"Oh, [bleep].

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.743
And this is
a historical election.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:25.678
And you got to be involved."

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.822 line:0%
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:07:36.822 --> 00:07:41.394 line:0%
-"Siempre, Luis"
is on HBO and HBO Max now.

